{#
  Migrations 0115 / 0116 — THE MERGE GATE FUNCTIONS.

  Authority: ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 function 7, §6.5, §16 (MI02, MI06, MI22, MI30);
  spec/errors.md §2.5 and §3.3; docs/leads/kernel.md rulings D4 (no CASE), D5, MR-8
  (0115/0116 — 0111-0114 are recall's and are occupied on disk).

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ONE TEMPLATE, TWO SUBJECTS. THAT IS THE THESIS, NOT A SAVING.
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  `MI02` ("a merged permit has zero open blocking checks") and `MI30` ("a change
  request merges only with zero open blocking checks — the repository is a protected
  branch") are the same invariant instantiated on two subjects. Writing them as two
  hand-authored functions would let them drift into two different claims, and the
  drift would be invisible: both files would still look correct. So this template
  emits both, from the binding's own `[[subject]]` declarations, and every name it
  needs is derived from `id_column`:

      permit_id → permit → permit_event, permit_clause, fn_permit_merge_gate
      cr_id     → cr     → cr_event,     cr_clause,     fn_cr_merge_gate

  That derivation is a CONVENTION, not a guess, and it is guarded by a test that
  reads both committed bindings and asserts the declared `event_table` agrees with
  it: `packages/trappoint-core/tests/test_binding_conventions.py`. It exists because
  `render._context()` does not yet expose `event_table`, `completion_table`,
  `epoch_pin_constraint` or `obligation_sources` to a template. See that test and the
  cross-domain note filed with this worker's output.

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  WHAT THIS FUNCTION MAY REFUSE, AND WHAT IT MUST LEAVE ALONE
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  `spec/errors.md` §3.3 is normative and it is the reason this function looks smaller
  than ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 function 7 does:

    * a synthetic `23514` raised from procedural code carries NO CONSTRAINT NAME, and
      the constraint name is the exhibit. §5.11's `RAISE ... ERRCODE='23514'` predates
      the SQLSTATE contract; conformance case CF-01 pins the correct behaviour in one
      line — "the trigger must NOT pre-empt the CHECK: the exhibit is the constraint
      name" — and asserts `23514` on `gate_closed_when_issued`, which only the real
      CHECK can produce.
    * so a non-zero obligation counter is refused by the counter's own CHECK, on the
      UPDATE this trigger has just returned NEW for, and this function says nothing.

  What is left is exactly the set of conditions no CHECK can hold, which is what
  `P0001` is reserved for (`spec/errors.md` §2.5):

    1. DRIFT and TIME (CF-02, CF-03) — the anti-join re-derivation disagrees with the
       projected counter. `now()` is not immutable, so an expired disposition can
       never be a CHECK; and a counter forced to zero out of band is by definition a
       value no CHECK over that counter can catch.
    2. ABSENCE of an authority-source row for a cited clause (CF-06) — FAIL CLOSED. A
       stale closure refusing while a missing one admitted was the original bug, and
       it is the one with physical consequences.
    3. ABSENCE of a boundary certificate (CF-53) — an asset with no modelled energy
       edges is UNKNOWN, not SAFE. `unmodelled_asset_count = 0` on a permit that was
       never certified is a certified null (I08), and it is indistinguishable from a
       clean certificate to every CHECK that can be written over it.

  The drift arm fires ONLY when the projected counter reads zero. When both the
  re-derivation and the counter are non-zero the counter's CHECK refuses with its
  name, and pre-empting it would trade a named exhibit for an unnamed one.

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  PLATFORM GROUND TRUTH MEASURED FOR THIS FILE (CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, local node)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  * READING a trigger row requires the PARENTHESISED form `(NEW).col`. `NEW.col` in a
    read position fails at CREATE TRIGGER time with `42P01: no data source matches
    prefix: new in this context` (cockroachdb#114687). WRITING `NEW.col := …` is fine
    unparenthesised. The same applies to the trigger's `WHEN` clause — see 0130.
  * `SELECT count(*) … FOR SHARE` is REFUSED with `0A000: FOR SHARE is not allowed
    with aggregate functions`. ARCHITECTURE.md §6.5 is written that way and does not
    run. The locking read is therefore a sub-select and the aggregate sits outside it.
    This function takes no lock at all: it is a BEFORE trigger on the row the caller
    already holds, and the locks that matter are the procedure's (0117/0118).
  * PL/pgSQL restrictions held: no loop, no EXECUTE, no PERFORM, no CASE (ruling D4);
    `IF`/`ELSIF` plus aggregate `SELECT … INTO`.
  * **THE P0001 EXHIBIT IS NOT RECOVERABLE FROM THE DRIVER ON THIS PLATFORM.** Measured
    through psycopg 3.3.4 against v26.2.5: a PL/pgSQL `RAISE` arrives with
    `diag.constraint_name = None` (expected, spec/errors.md §3.1) AND
    `diag.context = None` — there is no PL/pgSQL context stack — and
    `diag.source_function` is a CockroachDB Go internal (`func397`), which names
    nothing a courtroom could use. `spec/errors.md` §2.5 says the message MUST make
    the raising object recoverable, and on this platform the message is the ONLY
    channel that can. So every `RAISE` below names its own object:

        <PREFIX>: merge refused by <schema>.<object> — <what and why>

    which keeps §3.2's shape (one lower-case sentence, no trailing full stop) and lets
    `trappoint_core.errors.diagnose()` recover the exhibit the conformance manifest
    asserts. A refusal whose object name could only be inferred from the prefix is
    reported as WEAKENED rather than silently guessed.
#}
{% set ns = namespace(closure=none) %}
{% for a in authority_sources %}
{% if 'blocking_check.severity' in a.projects %}
{% set ns.closure = a %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if ns.closure is none %}
{#- A binding with no authority source for `blocking_check.severity` cannot render a
    gate: rule P-2 says the gate reads only projections, and a projection with no
    declared authority is the exact build error the Authority Source Contract exists to
    produce. StrictUndefined turns the next line into a TemplateRefused naming it. -#}
{{ this_binding_declares_no_authority_source_for_blocking_check_severity }}
{% endif %}
{% for s in subjects %}
{% set stem = s.id_column[:-3] %}
{% set number = 115 + loop.index0 %}
{% set counter = (s.counters | selectattr('column', 'equalto', 'open_blocking') | list
                  or s.counters | selectattr('polarity', 'equalto', 'zero_when_complete') | list)
                 | first %}
{% set cert_counter = (s.counters | selectattr('column', 'equalto', 'unmodelled_asset_count')
                       | list) %}
{% set certifies = binding.emit_outbox and (cert_counter | length) > 0 %}
-- @file 0{{ number }}_fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate.sql
{{ header(file='0' ~ number ~ '_fn_' ~ stem ~ '_merge_gate.sql',
          title='CREATE FUNCTION ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.fn_' ~ stem ~ '_merge_gate() — the re-derivation, the time condition, and the certified null',
          rationale='A gate that trusted its own counters would be a gate that a single bad UPDATE disarms, so the completing transition re-derives the open obligation count from the base tables and refuses when the derivation disagrees with the projection. It refuses nothing a CHECK can refuse: a synthetic 23514 carries no constraint name and the constraint name is the exhibit. What is left is exactly what no CHECK can hold — a condition over now(), a condition over the absence of a row, and a counter that was never computed at all.',
          mi=['MI02', 'MI22'] + (['MI06'] if certifies else []),
          i=['I02'] + (['I08'] if certifies else []),
          counsel_gated=false) }}
--
-- SUBJECT: {{ s.kind }} ({{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }}), completing state
-- '{{ s.completing_state }}'. Welded to the table by migration
-- 0{{ 130 + loop.index0 }}_trg_{{ stem }}_merge_gate.sql, which carries the WHEN clause that
-- restricts this function to the completing transition and keeps the trigger graph
-- acyclic and depth 1.
--
-- REFUSAL DEPTH, honestly stated. Every arm below is DEPTH 1 on its own: it is a
-- RAISE, and a RAISE is one mechanism. The depth-2 claim the conformance suite makes
-- for CF-01 and CF-31 is not made by this function — it is made by the counter's
-- CHECK ({{ counter.constraint }}) surviving when this trigger is disabled, and by
-- this trigger surviving when the CHECK is dropped. That claim is proved by the
-- unwelding matrix, not asserted here, and at runtime the CHECK is what fires
-- because this function deliberately declines to pre-empt it.
--
-- THE EXHIBIT for every refusal below is the fully-qualified name of this function
-- ({{ binding.schema }}.fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate), because `diag.constraint_name` is empty for
-- P0001 (spec/errors.md §3.1). A client recovers it from the PL/pgSQL error context;
-- where the driver cannot supply it, `trappoint_core.errors` records that the
-- diagnosis was WEAKENED rather than silently inferring it.

CREATE FUNCTION {{ binding.schema }}.fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate() RETURNS TRIGGER
LANGUAGE PLpgSQL AS $fn$
DECLARE
  v_subject   UUID;
  v_projected INT8;
  v_derived   INT8;
  v_unbacked  INT8;
{% if certifies %}
  v_certified INT8;
{% endif %}
BEGIN
  -- `(NEW).col`, not `NEW.col`. See the platform note in this file's template: the
  -- unparenthesised read form does not survive CREATE TRIGGER on v26.2.5.
  v_subject   := (NEW).{{ s.id_column }};
  v_projected := (NEW).{{ counter.column }};

  -- ── 1 · RE-DERIVE THE OPEN OBLIGATION COUNT FROM THE BASE TABLES ──────────────
  -- The projection is enforced, never trusted (rule P-2). An obligation is OPEN when
  -- no live disposition covers it, and "live" carries a time condition — `retracted_by
  -- IS NULL` is a fact a CHECK could hold, `expires_at > now()` is not.
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_derived
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check bc
   WHERE bc.{{ s.id_column }} = v_subject
     AND NOT EXISTS (
           SELECT 1 FROM {{ binding.schema }}.disposition d
            WHERE d.check_id = bc.check_id
              AND d.retracted_by IS NULL
              AND (d.expires_at IS NULL OR d.expires_at > now()));

  -- ── 2 · DRIFT AND TIME (CF-02, CF-03) ────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Only when the projection reads ZERO. If the counter is also non-zero then
  -- {{ counter.constraint }} refuses this write with its own name attached, and
  -- trading that named exhibit for an unnamed P0001 would be a strictly worse
  -- refusal (spec/errors.md §3.3, corollary).
  IF v_derived <> 0 AND v_projected = 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE = 'P0001',
      MESSAGE = '{{ binding.name }}: merge refused by {{ binding.schema }}.fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate'
                || ' — re-derived open obligation count is '
                || v_derived::STRING || ' while the projected counter reads zero';
  END IF;

  -- ── 3 · FAIL CLOSED ON AN ABSENT AUTHORITY-SOURCE ROW (CF-06) ────────────────
  -- Declared by this binding, not known to the substrate:
  --   authority {{ ns.closure.relation }} ({{ ns.closure.key_columns | join(', ') }})
  --          <= cited ({{ ns.closure.key | join(', ') }})
  --   on_missing raise
  -- Absence of evidence refuses. The original bug was the other asymmetry: a stale
  -- closure refused while a missing one admitted, and the missing one is the case
  -- with physical consequences.
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_unbacked
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.{{ stem }}_clause cited
   WHERE cited.{{ s.id_column }} = v_subject
     AND NOT EXISTS (
           SELECT 1 FROM {{ ns.closure.relation }} auth
            WHERE {% for column in ns.closure.key_columns %}auth.{{ column }} = cited.{{ ns.closure.key[loop.index0] }}{{ '
              AND ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %});
  IF v_unbacked <> 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE = 'P0001',
      MESSAGE = '{{ binding.name }}: merge refused by {{ binding.schema }}.fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate'
                || ' — no authority-source row for '
                || v_unbacked::STRING || ' cited clause version(s)';
  END IF;
{% if certifies %}

  -- ── 4 · THE CERTIFIED NULL (CF-53, invariant I08) ────────────────────────────
  -- {{ cert_counter[0].column }} = 0 means one of two things and they are not the same
  -- fact: CERTIFIED ZERO, or NEVER COMPUTED. No CHECK over that column can tell them
  -- apart, because both are the integer 0. So the gate demands the certificate row
  -- itself, and a {{ s.kind }} whose boundary was never certified is refused rather than
  -- admitted. CF-52 — a certificate that exists and reports unmodelled assets — is a
  -- different refusal and belongs to {{ cert_counter[0].constraint }}, which this
  -- function does not pre-empt.
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_certified
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.boundary_certificate cert
   WHERE cert.{{ s.id_column }} = v_subject;
  IF v_certified = 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE = 'P0001',
      MESSAGE = '{{ binding.name }}: merge refused by {{ binding.schema }}.fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate'
                || ' — no boundary certificate for this {{ s.kind }}';
  END IF;
{% else %}

  -- ── 4 · THE CERTIFIED NULL — NOT RENDERED FOR THIS BINDING ───────────────────
  -- This arm reads {{ binding.schema }}.boundary_certificate, which this binding does not
  -- declare and does not own (`emit_outbox = {{ 'true' if binding.emit_outbox else 'false' }}`, ruling D9). Stored
  -- procedures and functions bind EARLY on v26.2, so naming an absent relation here
  -- would make this migration un-appliable rather than make it degrade — which is
  -- exactly why the switch is at RENDER time and the fallback is this committed
  -- comment a reviewer can read (ruling D5). Conformance case CF-53 carries
  -- `profiles = ["mainline"]` for the same reason.
{% endif %}

  RETURN NEW;
END $fn$;
{% endfor %}
